   "note": "Stall torque falls in proportion to voltage. The STS servos cut out near 4 to 5 V by default [A]. Feed each arm's 12 V bus from its own buck regulator (about 10 A peak per arm) so one arm's stall cannot pull the others down; the pack sags from 25 V to about 21 V under hover load, which a buck converter absorbs."
  },
  "overload": {
   "note": "Feetech STS servos have torque limit, protection current and overload protection time registers. In flight a servo that trips goes limp, which is worse than running hot, so for joints 0 to 4 set the overload torque to 100 % and the protection time to its maximum, and let the flight controller's own current watch decide. For walking, a 80 % torque limit makes the arm give way before the gears strip. Metal gear trains in this size back-drive around 1.5 x stall [A]; above that teeth chip."
  }
